@lauracarlson 
Should we send comments to authors of the Tutorial (EOWG)? It looks it'll take long time for WCAG 3 will become the W3C recommendation. People will refer to the Tutorial to provide more appropriate alt text for images until then. If there is the issue in the "Decorative Images" document of the Tutorial, it is the issue of the Tutorial. we should contact EOWG and modify it at first. And then we can copy and paste it to the Method document for "Decorative Images".

@Myndex  @GreggVan Please can you specifically comment on my proposal for a universal approach to dealing with font-weight, via pixel density, using something similar to the approach described on page 11 of this paper https://visualstress.info/2020-254.pdf by Arnold Wilkins. 

Since my last post, I thought on the topic a bit further, and realised that basing it on the phrase "the quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog" means that the approach doesn't readily generalise to other languages with other alphabets. So, in order to provide a globally viable equivalent approach, I'm now proposing to do the pixel count by simply taking every letter in the alphabet, with no spaces in between, so calculate the pixel density of "abcdedfghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz", after cropping to the x-height. On this basis, we can give values of this font density for a few common fonts, and give ranges of this font density that are appropriate for body paragraphs of text, and separately, for individual words and short phrases.

I will look forward to further discussion on this topic, thanks.

