I do not believe "How-To" best describes the content of the page for each guideline as this heading is a synonym of "Method" and implies this is how to meet this particular guideline. However, once on the How-To pages, because it has [no children](https://github.com/w3c/silver/issues/318) a user cannot find the actual Methods without going back up to the WCAG 3.0 Guidelines; which is complicated to understand and therefore could be a barrier to entry.

I believe an easier mental model would be to rename "How-To" to "Overview" and have it as either a sibling or parent of the Outcomes for that guideline. 

- This should remove the confusion of what the purpose of this page is;
- It may also help with navigation problems reported in https://github.com/w3c/silver/issues/318 by moving towards a single tree;
- By not funneling less accessible/technical readers away from the Outcomes and Methods, and could help with adoption - especially for designers and engineers who are just learning about the subject.

