comments.body:
@GreggVan 

If we do not relate our work to common practice, on what basis do we connect this work to the reality we seek to improve?

While others may indeed adopt our work, our task and the only formal authority we have is to develop guidance that W3C can offer to those others and the world at large. How we describe that guidance doesn't create more authority.

@alastc, although the current draft uses placeholders, the technical architecture of **APCA** currently under evaluation uses **16px** as the baseline in its **Lookup Tables** to ensure fluent text readability.

Regarding the tests you mentioned that contradict my laboratory, it would be necessary for you to **share the methodology and the data** so they can be contrasted, if you wish.

Finally, and with this I conclude until next week's outcome: I also mentioned the **IEEE standard** itself.

@chaals, skimming the thread, I think I missed a bit about you suggesting a minimum text-size setting, rather than an increased text-size setting.

That is slightly different, but does have similar down-sides to the text-size-only adjustments.

If some parts of the content expand when others don't, there are simply more variables to account for in the layout. It isn't impossible, but when you go down that route you end up setting all the expandable things to the same unit, e.g. REMs. At that stage, you're just re-inventing zoom...
